'''Sarah Elizabeth Huckabee Sanders''' ({{née}} '''Huckabee'''; born August 13, 1982)<ref name="SHSbirthdate" /> is an American politician serving as the 47th [[governor of Arkansas]] since 2023. Sanders is the daughter of Ambassador [[Mike Huckabee]], who served from 1996 to 2007 as Arkansas's 44th governor.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Schonfeld |first1=Zach |title=Sarah Sanders wins Arkansas governor's mansion |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/3721608-sarah-sanders-wins-arkansas-governors-mansion/ |access-date=November 8, 2022 |work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |publisher=[[Nexstar Media Group]] |date=November 8, 2022}}</ref> A member of the [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican Party]], she was the 31st [[White House press secretary]], serving under President [[Donald Trump]] from 2017 to 2019. Sanders was the third woman to be White House press secretary.<ref name=":6">{{Cite web |title=Governor Sanders |url=https://governor.arkansas.gov/administration/governor-sanders/ |access-date=January 13, 2023 |website=Arkansas Governor - Sarah Huckabee Sanders |language=en-US}}</ref> She also served as a senior advisor on [[Donald Trump 2016 presidential campaign|Trump's 2016 presidential campaign]]. Sanders became the [[Republican Party of Arkansas|Republican]] nominee in the [[2022 Arkansas gubernatorial election]] and won, defeating Democratic nominee [[Chris Jones (Arkansas politician)|Chris Jones]]. She is running for reelection in [[2026 Arkansas gubernatorial election|2026]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Governor Sarah Huckabee Sanders Officially Files for Re-Election|url=https://www.sarahforgovernor.com/2025/11/03/governor-sarah-huckabee-sanders-officially-files-for-re-election/|website=Sarah Huckabee Sanders for Arkansas Governor|access-date=2026-01-30|language=en-US}}</ref>

As press secretary, Sanders was the spokesperson for the first Trump administration's policy decisions, and had a confrontational relationship with the [[White House press corps]].<ref>Multiple sources:
*{{Cite news|last=Grynbaum|first=Michael M.|date=June 14, 2019|title=Sarah Huckabee Sanders, Steadfast Trump Fan, Never Wavered|language=en-US|work=[[The New York Times]]|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/06/14/business/media/sarah-sanders-trump.html|access-date=September 25, 2020|issn=0362-4331}}
*{{Cite web|first=Tasneem|last=Nashrulla|title=White House Press Secretary Sarah Sanders Is Leaving The White House|url=https://www.buzzfeednews.com/article/tasneemnashrulla/sarah-sanders-out-press-secretary-white-house|date=June 13, 2019|access-date=September 25, 2020|website=[[BuzzFeed News]]|language=en}}
*{{Cite magazine|last=Williams|first=Paige|title=Is Sarah Huckabee Sanders the Future of the Republican Party?|url=https://www.newyorker.com/news/news-desk/is-sarah-huckabee-sanders-the-future-of-the-republican-party|date=June 17, 2019|access-date=September 25, 2020|magazine=[[The New Yorker]]|language=en-us}}
*{{Cite news|date=June 13, 2019|first=Noah|last=Bierman|title=Sarah Huckabee Sanders, Trump's White House press secretary, is stepping down|url=https://www.latimes.com/politics/la-na-pol-trump-sarah-sanders-resign-press-secretary-20190613-story.html|access-date=September 25, 2020|newspaper=[[The Los Angeles Times]]|language=en-US}}</ref> When interviewed by investigators as part of the [[Mueller special counsel investigation|Mueller probe]], she admitted making false statements in her role.<ref name=":4">{{Cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/19/us/politics/sarah-sanders-mueller-report.html|title=Sanders's 'Slip of the Tongue' Would Be a Problem in Some White Houses. Not Trump's.|last1=Karni|first1=Annie|date=April 19, 2019|work=[[The New York Times]]|access-date=April 20, 2019|last2=Haberman|first2=Maggie|author-link2=Maggie Haberman|language=en-US|issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref name=":3">{{Cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/04/18/us/politics/white-house-mueller-report.html|title=A Portrait of the White House and Its Culture of Dishonesty|last1=Baker|first1=Peter|date=April 18, 2019|work=[[The New York Times]]|access-date=April 20, 2019|last2=Haberman|first2=Maggie|author-link2=Maggie Haberman|language=en-US|issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref name=":5">{{Cite news|last=Smith|first=David|date=June 13, 2019|title=Sarah Sanders exits after two fraught years as Trump hails 'a very fine woman'|work=[[The Guardian]]|url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2019/jun/13/sarah-sanders-leaves-trump-white-house-stands-down-press-secretary-role|access-date=June 13, 2019}}</ref> Sanders hosted fewer press conferences than any of the 13 previous White House press secretaries.<ref>{{Cite news|date=June 13, 2019|title=White House press secretary Sarah Sanders to go|language=en-GB|work=[[BBC News]]|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-us-canada-48628233|access-date=September 25, 2020}}</ref>

In June 2019, Trump tweeted that Sanders would be leaving her role as press secretary.<ref name="ABC News">{{cite web|first=Jordyn|last=Phelps|url=https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/press-secretary-sarah-sanders-leaving-white-house/story?id=63696349|title=Press Secretary Sarah Sanders leaving White House|work=[[ABC News (United States)|ABC News]]|date=June 13, 2019}}</ref><ref name="nytimes.com">{{cite news|last1=Baker |first1=Peter |last2=Rogers |first2=Katie |title=Sarah Huckabee Sanders Leaving White House at the End of the Month |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/06/13/us/politics/sarah-sanders-leaving-white-house.html |newspaper=[[The New York Times]] |access-date=June 13, 2019 |date=June 13, 2019}}</ref> On January 25, 2021, she announced her candidacy for governor of Arkansas; Trump endorsed her. She secured the Republican nomination in May 2022; her general election opponents were the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic]] nominee, Chris Jones, and the [[Libertarian Party (United States)|Libertarian]] nominee, Ricky Dale Harrington. She is the first [[List of female governors in the United States|female governor]] of Arkansas, the first woman to be governor of a state of which her father was also governor,<ref name="auto">{{Cite web |date=November 8, 2022 |title=Sarah Huckabee Sanders 1st woman elected Arkansas governor |url=https://apnews.com/article/2022-midterm-elections-donald-trump-arkansas-congress-bca3dda7f9e6132bbac04b002b8c2825 |access-date=November 9, 2022 |website=[[Associated Press|AP News]] |language=en}}</ref><ref name="auto1">{{Cite web|url=https://www.cnn.com/2022/11/08/politics/sarah-huckabee-sanders-elected-governor-arkansas/index.html|title=CNN Projection: Republican Sarah Huckabee Sanders will win Arkansas governorship &#124; CNN Politics|first=Maureen|last=Chowdhury|date=November 9, 2022|website=[[CNN]]}}</ref> and the youngest current governor.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://arktimes.com/arkansas-blog/2022/11/22/sanders-announces-co-chairs-of-her-inauguration|title=Sanders announces co-chairs of her inauguration|first=Lindsey|last=Millar|date=November 22, 2022}}</ref>

Sanders has been recognized in ''[[Fortune (magazine)|Fortune]]'' and ''[[Time (magazine)|Time]]'' magazine's "40 under 40". She is the author of ''[[The New York Times]]'' bestseller ''Speaking for Myself'', is a former [[Fox News Channel]] contributor, and served on the [[Fulbright Program|Fulbright]] board.<ref name=":6" />

==Early life and education==
Sarah Elizabeth Huckabee was born on August 13, 1982, in [[Little Rock, Arkansas]].<ref name="SHSbirthdate">{{cite news |last1=Pruden |first1=William |title=10 Sarah Huckabee Sanders |url=https://encyclopediaofarkansas.net/entries/sarah-huckabee-sanders-13510/ |access-date=September 26, 2025 |work=[[Encyclopedia of Arkansas]] |date=September 26, 2025}}</ref>{{Better source needed|date=November 2025}} She is the youngest child and only daughter of [[Mike Huckabee]] and [[Janet Huckabee]] (''née'' McCain), both politicians.<ref name=Star>{{cite news |last=Gutierrez |first=Lisa |date=May 11, 2017 |title=Nine things to know about Sarah Huckabee Sanders, deputy White House press secretary |url=http://www.kansascity.com/news/politics-government/article149978072.html |access-date=May 12, 2017 |work=[[The Kansas City Star]] |publisher=[[The McClatchy Company]] |location=[[Kansas City, Missouri]]}}</ref><ref name="Heavy">{{Cite news |url=https://heavy.com/news/2017/05/sarah-huckabee-sanders-mike-daughter-father-parents-where-is-sean-spicer-bernie-related/ |title=Yes, Sarah Huckabee Sanders Is Mike Huckabee's Daughter|last=McBride|first=Jessica|date=May 11, 2017|work=[[Heavy.com]]|access-date=May 17, 2017}}</ref> She has two brothers, John Mark Huckabee and David Huckabee.<ref name=Heavy/> After graduating from [[Little Rock Central High School]] in [[Little Rock, Arkansas]],<ref name=AT2>{{cite web |url=http://www.arktimes.com/ArkansasBlog/archives/2017/01/19/sarah-huckabee-sanders-to-be-deputy-white-house-press-secretary |title=Sarah Huckabee Sanders to be deputy White House press secretary|date=January 19, 2017|work=[[Arkansas Times]]|first=Max|last=Brantley|access-date=March 5, 2017}}</ref> Huckabee attended [[Ouachita Baptist University]] (her father's alma mater) in [[Arkadelphia, Arkansas]]. She was elected [[Student-body president|student body president]] of the university and was active in Republican organizations. In 2004, she graduated from the university with a Bachelor of Arts degree, majoring in [[political science]] and minoring in [[mass communication]]s.<ref name=AT2/><ref name=Star/><ref name="ouachitadegree">{{Cite web |url=https://www.arkansasbaptist.org/read/sarah-huckabee-sanders-from-ouachita-to-the-white-house |title=Sarah Huckabee Sanders: From Ouachita to the White House |last=Colson |first=Margaret |website=Arkansas Baptist News |date=September 11, 2017 |access-date=May 4, 2018 |archive-date=November 17, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181117232317/https://www.arkansasbaptist.org/read/sarah-huckabee-sanders-from-ouachita-to-the-white-house |url-status=dead }}</ref>

==Early political career==
[[File:Mike Huckabee and Sarah Huckabee Sanders.jpg|thumb|left|Huckabee Sanders, center, and her father [[Mike Huckabee]] with [[U.S. Secretary of Education]] [[Margaret Spellings]] in 2005]]
Before entering politics as a candidate, Sanders served as a [[political consultant]] and a campaign insider. She was involved in her father's first campaign for the [[United States Senate]] in 1992. Describing the unsuccessful bid in an interview for ''[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]'', she said: "He didn't really have much of a staff, so our family has been very engaged and very supportive of my dad. I was stuffing envelopes, I was knocking on doors, I was putting up yard signs."<ref name=Heavy/> Her father said of her childhood, "I always say that when most kids are seven or eight years old out jumping rope, she was sitting at the kitchen table listening to political commentators analyze poll results." Huckabee said that he and his wife spoiled Sarah at times. He called her "doggone tough" and "fearless" due to having grown up with two brothers.<ref name=":1">{{Cite news|url=https://www.foxnews.com/politics/who-is-sarah-huckabee-sanders-how-her-family-prepped-her-for-the-white-house-briefing-room/|title=Who is Sarah Huckabee Sanders? How her family prepped her for the White House briefing room|last=Schallhorn |first=Kaitlyn|date=May 12, 2017|work=[[Fox News]]|access-date=May 16, 2017}}</ref> Sanders was a field coordinator for her father's [[Arkansas gubernatorial election, 2002|2002 reelection campaign]] for [[governor of Arkansas]]. She was a regional liaison for congressional affairs at the [[United States Department of Education|U.S. Department of Education]] under President [[George W. Bush]],<ref name="JLemireAP07222017" /> and worked as a field coordinator for Bush's reelection campaign in [[Ohio]] in 2004.<ref name="archive.org">{{cite web|url=http://www.tsamoutales.com/team |title=Team |date=May 26, 2015 |publisher=Tsamoutales Strategies |location=[[Tallahassee, Florida]] |access-date=July 22, 2017 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150526190741/http://www.tsamoutales.com/team |archive-date=May 26, 2015 }}</ref>

Sanders was a founding partner of Second Street Strategies in Little Rock, a general consulting services provider<ref name="2ndStStratArchive">{{cite web |url=http://www.secondstreetstrategies.com/about/ |title=About Us: Sarah Huckabee Sanders |date=September 13, 2016 |publisher=Second Street Strategies |location=[[Little Rock, Arkansas]] |access-date=July 22, 2017 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160913043747/http://www.secondstreetstrategies.com/about |archive-date=September 13, 2016}}</ref> for Republican campaigns.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.secondstreetstrategies.com/services|title=Services: Campaigns|publisher=Second Street Strategies |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160913083041/http://www.secondstreetstrategies.com/services|archive-date=September 13, 2016 |url-status=dead|access-date=September 19, 2017}}</ref> She worked on national political campaigns and campaigns for federal office in Arkansas. Sanders was also vice president of [[Frank Tsamoutales|Tsamoutales Strategies]].<ref name="archive.org" /> She was national political director for her father's [[Mike Huckabee presidential campaign, 2008|2008 presidential campaign]].<ref name=":6" /> After her father's 2008 campaign, she worked as executive director of Huck PAC, a [[political action committee]].<ref name="archive.org" /> She was also a senior adviser to [[Tim Pawlenty]] in his 2012 presidential run. Sanders was involved in the campaigns of both U.S. senators from Arkansas, managing [[John Boozman]]'s 2010 campaign and serving as a senior adviser to [[Tom Cotton]]'s 2014 campaign. 

In 2010, Sanders was named one of ''[[Time (magazine)|Time]]'' magazine's "40 under 40" in politics.<ref>{{cite magazine |url=https://content.time.com/time/specials/packages/article/0,28804,2023831_2023829_2025232,00.html|title=40 Under 40|magazine=[[Time (magazine)|Time]]|date=October 14, 2010|access-date=March 27, 2017|via=content.time.com}}</ref> She served for a period as the national campaign manager for the [[ONE Campaign]], a global nonprofit founded by Irish musician [[Bono]] aimed at ending [[global poverty]] and [[preventable disease]]s.<ref name="archive.org" /><ref name="2ndStStratArchive" /> In 2016, after managing [[Mike Huckabee 2016 presidential campaign|her father's presidential campaign]], Sanders signed on as a senior adviser for [[Donald Trump presidential campaign, 2016|Donald Trump's 2016 presidential campaign]] and ran its communications for coalitions.<ref>{{cite web|title=Huckabee's daughter joins Trump team; Rubio to campaign with Hutchinson |url=http://www.arkansasnews.com/news/arkansas/huckabee-s-daughter-joins-trump-team-rubio-campaign-hutchinson |work=arkansasnews.com/|date= February 25, 2016|access-date=February 3, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title=Sarah Huckabee Sanders joining Trump's communications staff|url=https://thehill.com/blogs/ballot-box/presidential-races/294464-sarah-huckabee-sanders-joining-trumps-communications/|work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]|date=September 4, 2016|access-date=February 3, 2017}}</ref>

==Trump administration (2017–2019)==
[[file:Sarah Sanders 2017.jpg|thumb|right|Sanders in 2017 while White House deputy press secretary]]
After Trump was elected, Sanders was named to the position of deputy White House press secretary in his new administration. On May 5, 2017, she held her first White House press briefing, standing in for Press Secretary [[Sean Spicer]], who was serving on [[United States Naval Reserve|Naval Reserve]] duty.<ref name="TheHill20170505">{{cite news |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/administration/332109-huckabee-sanders-pinch-hits-for-spicer-at-white-house/|title=Huckabee Sanders pinch hits for Spicer at White House|last1=Fabian|first1=Jordan|date=May 5, 2017|work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]|access-date=May 6, 2017}}</ref> She continued to cover for Spicer until his return to the podium on May 12. She stood in for Spicer during the [[dismissal of James Comey]] and the controversy following it. Her defense of the Trump administration's actions led to some speculation that Trump was considering promoting her to replace Spicer.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Hickey |first1=Jennifer G. |title=Is Sarah Huckabee Sanders 'auditioning' for bigger White House role? |url=https://www.foxnews.com/politics/is-sarah-huckabee-sanders-auditioning-for-bigger-white-house-role |date=May 12, 2017 |publisher=[[Fox News]] |access-date=May 12, 2017}}</ref> This was denied at the time by her father.<ref name="TheHill20170512">{{cite news |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/administration/333207-huckabee-my-daughter-has-no-desire-for-spicers-job/|title=Huckabee: Daughter 'has no desire' to take Spicer's job|last1=Hensch|first1=Mark|date=May 12, 2017|work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]|access-date=May 14, 2017}}</ref> But on May 26, ''[[The Wall Street Journal]]'' again suggested that Sanders was being considered as a possible replacement for Spicer, in the context of wider staff changes and the investigation into alleged communications with Russia.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://thehill.com/policy/technology/335374-white-house-considering-vetting-trumps-tweets-report/|title=White House considering vetting Trump's tweets|last1=Easley|first1=Jonathan|date=May 27, 2017|work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |access-date=May 27, 2017}}</ref> She continued to fill in for Spicer occasionally.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.foxnews.com/politics/sarah-huckabee-sanders-replaces-spicer-as-white-house-press-secretary|title=Sarah Huckabee Sanders replaces Spicer as White House press secretary|last=Pappas|first=Alex|date=July 21, 2017|work=[[Fox News]] |access-date=July 21, 2017}}</ref>

After Trump dismissed FBI director [[James Comey]] in May 2017, Sanders said that she "heard from countless members of the [[FBI]] that are grateful and thankful for the President's decision" to fire him. Emails show that several FBI heads of regional field offices and high-ranking FBI members reacted with dismay to Comey's firing.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/emails-indicate-comey-was-well-liked-by-fbi-staffers/|title=Emails indicate James Comey was well-liked by FBI staffers|last1=Shabab|first1=Rebecca|date=February 5, 2018|work=[[CBS News]]|access-date=June 15, 2018}}</ref> After Trump sought to discredit Comey and the FBI, Sanders was questioned on a tweet she had sent during the 2016 presidential election that "when you're attacking FBI agents because you're under criminal investigation, you're losing".<ref>{{cite web |title=Sarah Sanders challenged on pre-election tweet |date=April 13, 2018 |url=https://edition.cnn.com/videos/politics/2018/04/13/whpb-sarah-sanders-tweet-jim-acosta-exchange-sot.cnn |publisher=[[CNN]] |access-date=June 15, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.dailydot.com/layer8/sarah-sanders-fbi-tweet-trump/|title=Old Sarah Huckabee Sanders tweet about 'attacking FBI' goes viral after Trump's tweetstorm |last1=Wyrich |first1=Andrew|website=[[The Daily Dot]]|date=December 4, 2017 |access-date=June 15, 2018}}</ref> After Comey accused Trump of lying about the circumstances in which he was dismissed, Sanders defended Trump: "I can definitively say the president is not a liar, and I think it's frankly insulting that question would be asked."<ref>{{cite web |last1=Palmeri |first1=Tara |title=Sarah Sanders: 'The president is not a liar' |url=https://www.politico.com/story/2017/06/08/sarah-sanders-trump-comey-hearing-239301 |work=[[Politico]] |date=June 8, 2017 |access-date=June 15, 2018}}</ref>

On June 27, 2017, during a press briefing, Sanders criticized the media, accusing them of spreading "[[Fake news#Usage of the term by Donald Trump|fake news]]" about Trump. Sanders cited a video created by [[James O'Keefe]]. Although unsure of the video's accuracy, she said, "I would encourage everyone in this room and, frankly, everybody across the country to take a look at it." The video features [[CNN]]'s health and medical producer, John Bonifield, saying that CNN's coverage of the Trump campaign's alleged links to Russia are "mostly bullshit" and driven by ratings.<ref name="NYT">{{cite news |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2017/06/27/business/media/cnn-retracted-story-on-trump.html|title=A Costly Retraction for CNN and an Opening for Trump|last1=Grynbaum|first1=Michael M.|date=June 27, 2017|work=[[The New York Times]]|access-date=June 28, 2017}}</ref><ref name="FakeN">{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/the-fix/wp/2017/06/27/sarah-huckabee-sanders-lambastes-fake-news-while-promoting-journalist-known-for-deceptive-videos/|title=Sarah Huckabee Sanders lambastes fake news — and then promotes a journalist accused of deceptive videos|last1=Blake|first1=Aaron|date=June 27, 2017|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|access-date=June 28, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://abcnews.go.com/Entertainment/wireStory/latest-provocateur-targets-cnn-hidden-camera-video-48302829|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170627211717/http://abcnews.go.com/Entertainment/wireStory/latest-provocateur-targets-cnn-hidden-camera-video-48302829|url-status=dead|archive-date=June 27, 2017|title=The Latest: Sanders attacks CNN, media in briefing|date=June 27, 2017|agency=[[Associated Press]]|access-date=June 28, 2017}}</ref> Special counsel [[Robert Mueller]] found that Russia did interfere with the election, but "did not find that the Trump campaign, or anyone associated with it, conspired or coordinated with the Russian government in these efforts".<ref>{{Cite web |title=Mueller finds no collusion with Russia, leaves obstruction question open |url=https://www.americanbar.org/news/abanews/aba-news-archives/2019/03/mueller-concludes-investigation/ |access-date=January 13, 2023 |website=www.americanbar.org |language=en}}</ref> 

On June 29, 2017, Sanders said during a press briefing that the "president in no way, form or fashion has ever promoted or encouraged violence." In February 2016, Trump had said during a campaign speech: "So if you see somebody getting ready to throw a tomato, knock the crap out of them, would you?... I promise you, I will pay for the legal fees. I promise." [[PolitiFact]] also "found at least seven other examples in which Trump offered public musings that showed a tolerance for, and sometimes even a favorable disposition towards physical violence."<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.politifact.com/truth-o-meter/statements/2017/jul/05/sarah-huckabee-sanders/has-donald-trump-never-promoted-or-encouraged-viol/|title=Has Donald Trump never 'promoted or encouraged violence,' as Sarah Huckabee Sanders said?|last1=Jacobson|first1=Louis|last2=Tobias|first2=Manuela|date=July 5, 2017|publisher=[[PolitiFact]] |access-date=June 6, 2018}}</ref>

[[File:Secretary Tillerson Poses for a Photo With Senior U.S. and Chinese Government Leaders During a Tour of the Forbidden City in Beijing (37565580414).jpg|thumb|Sanders with [[Rex Tillerson]], [[Jared Kushner]], [[Robert Lighthizer]], and [[Wang Yi (politician)|Wang Yi]] in Beijing, China, November 2017]]
On July 21, 2017, after Spicer announced that he was going to resign, newly appointed [[White House Communications Director]] [[Anthony Scaramucci]] announced that Sanders would take the role of White House press secretary.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.cnn.com/2017/07/21/politics/sean-spicer-resigns-anthony-scaramucci/index.html|title=Sean Spicer, White House press secretary, resigns|last1=Diamond|first1=Jeremy|date=July 21, 2017|work=[[CNN]]|access-date=July 21, 2017|last2=Collins|first2=Kaitlan|last3=Zeleny|first3=Jeff|last4=Bash|first4=Dana}}</ref> Sanders is the third woman to hold the role of White House Press Secretary after [[Dee Dee Myers]] in 1993 and [[Dana Perino]] in 2007.<ref name="JLemireAP07222017">{{cite news |last1=Lemire |first1=Jonathan |last2=Lucey |first2=Catherine |date=July 22, 2017 |title=For Sanders, Path to Trump Press Secretary Began in Arkansas |url=https://apnews.com/58634f4bdff0443eb068492bf59fc66f |work=[[Associated Press]] |location=[[New York City]] |access-date=July 22, 2017 |quote=Arkansas-raised, Sanders is married to a Republican consultant and moved her young family to Washington to be part of the administration. |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170922002036/http://www.apnewsarchive.com/2017/For_Sanders%2C_path_to_Trump_press_secretary_began_in_Arkansas/id-58634f4bdff0443eb068492bf59fc66f |archive-date=September 22, 2017 }}</ref> Sanders is the first mother, and first working mother, to ever hold the position.<ref name=":6" />

In August 2017, Sanders said Trump "certainly didn't dictate" a statement released by [[Donald Trump Jr.]] about the [[Trump campaign–Russian meetings#Trump Tower meeting, June 9, 2016|2016 Trump Tower meeting with Russians]]. She also said that Trump "weighed in, offered suggestion like any father would do." In January 2018, Trump's lawyers wrote to the [[Special Counsel investigation (2017–present)|special counsel investigation]] that "the President dictated" the statement Donald Trump Jr. released. In June 2018, the media asked Sanders to explain the discrepancy in the statements, but she repeatedly refused to answer, saying: "I'm not going to respond to a letter from the president's outside counsel ... We've purposefully walled off, and I would refer you to them for comment", and "I'm an honest person".<ref>{{cite web |title=How Statements on Trump and Trump Tower Meeting Changed |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/how-statements-on-trump-tower-meeting-have-changed/ |agency=[[Associated Press]] |work=[[CBS News]]|date=June 5, 2018 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/administration/390607-sanders-dodges-questions-about-trump-tower-statement/|title=Sanders dodges questions about Trump Tower statement|last1=Fabian|first1=Jordan|date=June 4, 2018|work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |access-date=June 6, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Cummings |first1=William |title='I'm an honest person,' defensive Sarah Sanders says when grilled about credibility |url=https://www.9news.com/article/news/nation-now/im-an-honest-person-defensive-sarah-sanders-says-when-grilled-about-credibility/465-af80f6b2-06a2-4a84-8ef2-c8e5893172ce |work=[[USA Today]] |access-date=June 6, 2018 |archive-date=November 7, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211107030931/https://www.9news.com/article/news/nation-now/im-an-honest-person-defensive-sarah-sanders-says-when-grilled-about-credibility/465-af80f6b2-06a2-4a84-8ef2-c8e5893172ce |url-status=dead }}</ref>

In October 2017, [[CBS News]]'s Jacqueline Alemany asked Sanders whether the official White House position was that all 16 women who accused Trump of [[Donald Trump sexual misconduct allegations|sexual harassment]] were lying. Sanders responded, "Yeah, we've been clear on that since the beginning, and the President has spoken on it", without elaborating.<ref>{{cite news |author=<!--Staff writer(s); no by-line.--> |date=October 27, 2017 |title=White House: Women who accused Trump of sexual harassment are lying; Trump to announce Fed pick next week |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/watch-white-house-press-briefing-live-stream-2017-10-27/ |url-status=live |work=[[CBS News]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171227213402/https://www.cbsnews.com/news/watch-white-house-press-briefing-live-stream-2017-10-27/ |archive-date=December 27, 2017 |access-date=July 30, 2020 }}</ref> In November 2017, Trump [[Donald Trump on social media#Britain First videos|retweeted three unverified anti-Muslim videos]] by [[Britain First]], a far-right British group with a history of posting misleading videos. Sanders commented, "Whether it's a real video, the threat is real and that is what the president is talking about, that's what the president is focused on, is dealing with those real threats, and those are real no matter how you're looking at it." [[Theresa May]], the [[prime minister of the United Kingdom]], was among those to criticize Trump's actions.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Stracqualursi |first1=Veronica |last2=Travers |first2=Karen |last3=Phelps |first3=Jordyn |date=November 30, 2017 |title=White House defends Trump's retweets of far-right group |url=https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/trump-retweets-british-nationalist-anti-muslim-videos/story?id=51455324 |url-status=live |work=[[ABC News (United States)|ABC News]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200722220304/https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/trump-retweets-british-nationalist-anti-muslim-videos/story?id=51455324 |archive-date=July 22, 2020 |access-date=July 30, 2020 }}</ref>

In February 2018, after [[Rob Porter]] left the White House over domestic abuse allegations, Sanders said that Porter's background check was "ongoing, and the White House had not received any specific papers regarding the completion of that background check". After FBI director [[Christopher A. Wray|Christopher Wray]] testified that the FBI had finished and submitted its security-clearance investigation on Porter to the White House earlier in July 2017, Sanders instead claimed that it was instead the White House's personnel security office's investigation that was ongoing, which contradicted her earlier statement that the clearance process "doesn't operate within the White House".<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/the-fix/wp/2018/02/13/sarah-huckabee-sanders-pleads-ignorance-as-the-rob-porter-mess-worsens/|title=Sarah Huckabee Sanders pleads ignorance as the Rob Porter mess worsens|last1=Borchers|first1=Callum|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|access-date=June 15, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2018/02/13/fbi-contradicts-white-house-on-rob-porter-probe-timeline.html|title=FBI director contradicts White House timeline on Rob Porter abuse probe, says agency finished background check last summer|last1=Mangan|first1=Dan|work=[[CNBC]]|access-date=June 15, 2018|last2=Breuninger|first2=Kevin}}</ref> She said that Porter had made a "personal decision" to leave the White House, while White House deputy press secretary [[Raj Shah]] said that Porter was "terminated".<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.vox.com/2018/2/8/16993038/white-house-rob-porter-domestic-abuse-flip-flop|title=The White House press office changed its story on Rob Porter 3 times in one day|last1=Coaston|first1=Jane|date=February 8, 2018|work=[[Vox (website)|Vox]]|access-date=June 15, 2018}}</ref>

[[File:DW2eapcV4AUNn q.jpg|thumb|Sanders with [[Ivanka Trump]], [[Lauren Gibbs]], and [[Shauna Rohbock]] at the [[2018 Winter Olympics]] in [[2018 Winter Olympics|Pyeongchang]], South Korea]]
In March 2018, Sanders said of the [[Stormy Daniels–Donald Trump scandal]], "there was no knowledge of any payments from the president" to Daniels. In May 2018, Trump's lawyer [[Rudy Giuliani]] said that Trump had repaid his lawyer [[Michael Cohen (lawyer)|Michael Cohen]] $130,000 after Cohen paid Daniels. In response to questions about the discrepancy, Sanders claimed that she did not know of this development and that her earlier statement was based on the "best information" she had at the time.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://nypost.com/2018/05/03/sanders-i-didnt-know-about-trumps-hush-money-payment/|title=Sanders: I didn't know about Trump's 'hush money' payment|last1=Fredericks|first1=Bob|date=May 3, 2018|work=[[New York Post]]|access-date=June 15, 2018}}</ref> In mid-June 2018, when questioned on the Trump administration's [[Trump administration family separation policy|family separation policy]], which resulted in the separation of migrant children from their parents at the [[Mexico–United States border]], Sanders falsely blamed Democrats for the policy and said "it is very biblical" to implement the policy.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Higgins |first1=Tucker |title=White House on separating migrant children from parents: 'It's very biblical to enforce the law' |date=June 14, 2018 |url=https://www.cnbc.com/2018/06/14/white-house-on-separating-migrant-children-from-parents-its-very-biblical-to-enforce-the-law.html |publisher=[[CNBC]] |access-date=June 15, 2018}}</ref> Christian leaders such as Cardinal [[Daniel DiNardo]] and [[Franklin Graham]] strongly disagreed with the policy, calling it "immoral" or "disgraceful", while [[Bible scholars|Bible scholar]] and professor Matthew Schlimm said that the Bible was being misused just as slave traders and [[Nazism|Nazis]] had done.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.huffingtonpost.com/entry/jeff-sessions-trump-immigration-family-separation-border_us_5b23ff24e4b0783ae128f90d |title=Christian Leaders To Jeff Sessions: The Bible Does Not Justify Separating Families|first=Jennifer|last=Bendery|author-link=Jennifer Bendery|date=June 15, 2018|work=[[Huffington Post]]|access-date=June 16, 2018}}</ref>

In July 2018, Sanders said the Trump White House would discuss allowing Russian agents to interrogate former U.S. ambassador to Russia [[Michael McFaul]].<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/administration/397701-white-house-trump-open-to-russia-questioning-us-citizens/|title=White House says Trump to discuss allowing Russia to question US citizens|last=Thomsen|first=Jacqueline |date=July 18, 2018|work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]|access-date=July 19, 2018}}</ref> The Russian government had harassed and intimidated McFaul for years, without specifying what criminal allegations they would interrogate him about.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.politico.com/story/2018/07/18/trump-russia-browder-mcfaul-questioning-731616|title=White House: Trump will consider letting Russia question investor, former ambassador|last=Mueller|first=Eleanor|date=July 18, 2018|work=[[Politico]]|access-date=July 19, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/morning-mix/wp/2018/07/19/trump-putin-conversation-about-russian-interrogation-of-u-s-diplomat-prompts-outrage-astonishment/|title=Outrage erupts over Trump-Putin 'conversation' about letting Russia interrogate ex-U.S. diplomat Michael McFaul|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|language=en|access-date=July 19, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2018-07-18/putin-asked-trump-to-question-obama-s-ambassador-sanders-says |title=Putin Asked Trump to Let Russia Question McFaul, White House Says|work=[[Bloomberg News|Bloomberg]]|access-date=July 19, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2018/jul/19/trump-putin-interrogate-us-ambassador-michael-mcfaul|title='Absurd, crazy': Trump discussed allowing Putin to interrogate US ambassador|author=Guardian staff|last2=agencies|date=July 19, 2018 |website=[[The Guardian]]|access-date=July 19, 2018}}</ref> [[United States Department of State|U.S. State Department]] spokeswoman [[Heather Nauert]] said that she could not answer on the White House's behalf, but that the State Department considered the Russian allegations against McFaul "absolutely absurd".<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.cnbc.com/2018/07/18/russias-claims-about-americans-it-seeks-to-question-are-absurd-say.html|title=Russia's claims about Americans it seeks to question are 'absurd,' says State Department |last1=Breuninger|first1=Kevin|date=July 18, 2018|work=[[CNBC]]|access-date=July 19, 2018|last2=Wilkie|first2=Christina}}</ref> Several current and former diplomats condemned the White House's willingness to entertain Russian interrogation of a former U.S. ambassador.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.thedailybeast.com/us-officials-at-a-fucking-loss-over-latest-russia-sell-out|title=U.S. Officials 'at a Fucking Loss' Over Latest Russia Sellout|last=Ackerman|first=Spencer|date=July 18, 2018|work=[[The Daily Beast]]|access-date=July 19, 2018}}</ref>

In an August 2018 press conference, Sanders was repeatedly asked by [[Jim Acosta]], a CNN reporter, to say that the press was not the "enemy of the people", and she did not do so.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/sarah-sanders-press-briefing-white-house-jim-acosta-donald-trump-choke-enemy-of-people-a8475166.html|title=Sarah Sanders refuses to say press is not 'enemy of the people'|work=[[The Independent]]|access-date=August 2, 2018}}</ref> Sanders had a tumultuous relationship with the press and was the subject of numerous personal attacks regarding her looks and her weight.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Biden will deliver State of the Union address on Feb. 7 |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/biden-state-of-the-union-address-february-7/ |access-date=January 13, 2023 |website=[[CBS News]] |date=January 13, 2023 |language=en-US}}</ref> That same month, ''[[The Washington Post]]'' reported that Sanders and her deputy [[Bill Shine]] strategized optimal times to release announcements that the security clearances of various Trump critics and officials involved in the probe into Russian interference in the 2016 election had been revoked.<ref name=":0">{{Cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/former-intelligence-officials-rebuke-trump-for-pulling-brennans-security-clearance/2018/08/17/ea8382f2-a20d-11e8-8e87-c869fe70a721_story.html|title=White House drafts more clearance cancellations demanded by Trump|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|access-date=August 18, 2018}}</ref><ref name=":2">{{Cite news|url=https://thehill.com/homenews/administration/402437-sanders-shine-discussing-how-trump-revoking-security-clearances-can/|title=Trump aides discussed using security clearance revocations to distract from negative stories: report|last=Wise|first=Justin |date=August 17, 2018|work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]|access-date=August 18, 2018}}</ref> The announcements were intended to be released to distract from news cycles that were unfavorable to the White House.<ref name=":0" /><ref name=":2" />

The morning after publication of the September 5, 2018, ''[[The New York Times|New York Times]]'' op-ed "[[I Am Part of the Resistance Inside the Trump Administration]]", Sanders used her official government [[Twitter]] account to tweet<ref name="SandersTweet20180906">{{cite web |last1=Sanders |first1=Sarah |url=https://twitter.com/PressSec/status/1037728144642506752 |title=For those of you asking for the identity of the anonymous coward |website=@PressSec on Twitter |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180917173223/https://twitter.com/PressSec/status/1037728144642506752 |archive-date=September 17, 2018 |date=September 6, 2018 |url-status=live }}</ref> that the anonymous writer was a "gutless loser" and to charge that those in the newspaper's opinion department are "the only ones complicit in this deceitful act".<ref name=NewYorker20180924/> Her September 6 tweet gave the telephone number of the newspaper's opinion desk, and two former White House ethics chiefs declared that her tweet had violated federal law in an abuse of power, similar to her June 23, 2018, tweet specifically naming the restaurant that had refused her service in the [[Red Hen restaurant controversy]].<ref name=NewYorker20180924>{{cite magazine |last1=Williams |first1=Paige |title=Sarah Huckabee Sanders, Trump's Battering Ram |url=https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2018/09/24/sarah-huckabee-sanders-trumps-battering-ram |magazine=[[The New Yorker]] |date=September 24, 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180920023131/https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2018/09/24/sarah-huckabee-sanders-trumps-battering-ram |archive-date=September 20, 2018 |url-status=live}} Print edition title: "The Mouthpiece".</ref>
[[File:President Trump Welcome the Prime Minister of Israel to the White House, 25 March 2019.jpg|thumb|President Trump, joined by [[Benjamin Netanyahu|Netanyahu]], [[Mike Pence|Pence]], [[Jared Kushner|Kushner]], and Sanders, signs the proclamation which [[United States recognition of the Golan Heights as part of Israel|recognized]] Israel's 1981 [[Golan Heights Law|annexation]] of the [[Golan Heights]], March 2019.]]

In early November, CNN's Jim Acosta engaged in a verbal argument with Trump. While Acosta was asking Trump a question, an intern, at Trump's direction, tried to take away Acosta's microphone. Later in the day, Acosta's White House credentials were suspended, a move that was widely criticized as unprecedented.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.cnn.com/2018/11/08/media/trump-acosta-press-pass-reaction/index.html |title=Reporters condemn White House decision to bar CNN's Acosta |first=Brian |last=Stelter |date=November 8, 2018 |website=[[CNN Business]] |access-date=November 11, 2018}}</ref> The next day, to justify the White House's actions, Sanders released a video of the moment the intern tried to grab the microphone from Acosta's hand. The video originated from conspiracy theorist [[Paul Joseph Watson]] of the far-right website [[Infowars]], and was allegedly altered to make Acosta seem aggressive and excluded him saying, "Pardon me, ma'am", to the intern. Watson denied that the video was doctored in any way.<ref name="DailyDot">{{cite web |last1=Covucci |first1=David |title=Did Sarah Sanders post a doctored InfoWars video to justify CNN ban? |url=https://www.dailydot.com/layer8/sarah-sanders-jim-acosta-infowars/ |website=dailydot.com |publisher=Daily Dot |access-date=June 13, 2019 |date=June 13, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2018/11/08/white-house-shares-doctored-video-support-punishment-journalist-jim-acosta/|title=White House shares doctored video to support punishment of journalist Jim Acosta|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |access-date=March 7, 2019}}</ref> CNN Communications Executive called Sanders's sharing of the video "shameful" and the White House News Photographers Association said it was "appalled" by her actions, called video manipulation "deceptive, dangerous and unethical", and said that what Sanders did was "equally problematic".<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.msn.com/en-us/news/politics/white-house-shares-doctored-video-to-support-punishment-of-journalist-jim-acosta/ar-BBPuaAo?OCID=ansmsnnews11 |title=White House shares doctored video to support punishment of journalist Jim Acosta |first=Drew |last=Harwell |date=November 9, 2018 |website=[[MSN]] |access-date=November 11, 2018}}</ref>

During the [[2018–2019 United States federal government shutdown|2018–2019 federal government shutdown]] caused by Congress's refusal to fulfill Trump's demand for $5.7 billion in federal funds for a U.S.–Mexico border wall, Sanders argued that the wall was necessary, claiming that the CBP stopped nearly 4,000 known or suspected terrorists when they crossed the Mexico border in 2018. Data obtained by NBC News contradicted Sanders's assertion, showing that from October 1, 2017, to March 31, 2018, only six immigrants on the [[No Fly List]] (also known as the terror watch list) were encountered at ports of entry on the Mexico border.<ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/immigration/only-six-immigrants-terrorism-database-stopped-cbp-southern-border-first-n955861|title=Only six immigrants in terrorism database stopped by CBP at southern border from October to March|website=[[NBC News]] |date=January 7, 2019 |access-date=January 11, 2019}}</ref> In an interview with [[Chris Wallace]] on Fox News, Wallace countered her claim of nearly 4,000 terrorists, saying, "I know the statistic. I didn't know if you were going to use it, but I studied up on this. Do you know where those 4,000 people come—where they are captured? Airports."<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.thedailybeast.com/fox-news-chris-wallace-shuts-down-sarah-huckabee-sanders-claim-about-terrorists-crossing-border|title=Chris Wallace Shuts Down Sanders' Claim About Terrorists Crossing Border|last=Wilstein|first=Matt|date=January 6, 2019|work=[[The Daily Beast]]|access-date=January 11, 2019}}</ref> In January 2019 Sanders said on the [[Christian Broadcasting Network]] that she thinks "God calls all of us to fill different roles at different times, and I think that he wanted Donald Trump to become president".<ref>{{cite news |author=Brody, David |title=White House Press Secretary Sarah Sanders: 'God Wanted Donald Trump to Become President' |url=http://www1.cbn.com/cbnnews/politics/2019/january/exclusive-white-house-press-secretary-sarah-sanders-god-wanted-donald-trump-to-become-president |work=CBN News |date=January 30, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190131205452/http://www1.cbn.com/cbnnews/politics/2019/january/exclusive-white-house-press-secretary-sarah-sanders-god-wanted-donald-trump-to-become-president |archive-date=January 31, 2019 |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[File:Sarah Sanders last appearance as Press Secretary.jpg|thumb|Sanders, President [[Donald Trump]], and other White House staff members at her last appearance as White House press secretary on June 28, 2019]]
On June 13, 2019, Trump tweeted that Sanders would leave her role as press secretary on June 30.<ref name="ABC News"/><ref name="nytimes.com"/> Under Sanders, the White House set at least three records for the most days between formal press briefings. The White House had a 41-day streak that ended in January 2019, then a 42-day streak that ended in March 2019, followed by 94 days and counting without a formal press briefing when Sanders's departure was announced.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Hutzler |first1=Alexandra |title=Sarah Sanders has not had a formal press briefing in 43 days, setting a new White House record |url=https://www.newsweek.com/sarah-sanders-press-briefing-record-1403781 |website=[[Newsweek]] |date=April 23, 2019 |access-date=June 14, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Gilmour |first1=Jared |title=Press secretary Sarah Sanders is out — 94 days after her last White House briefing |url=https://www.miamiherald.com/news/nation-world/national/article231535588.html |website=[[The Miami Herald]] |access-date=June 14, 2019}}</ref>

=== Mueller report findings ===
On April 18, 2019, the first volume of the [[Mueller report]], the [[Mueller special counsel investigation|Special Counsel Investigation]] report compiled by [[Robert Mueller]], revealed that Sanders admitted that she had lied during a press conference when she said various things about [[James Comey]], the former FBI director. This included lying about the firing of former [[United States Attorney General|Attorney General]] [[Jeff Sessions]] and [[Deputy Attorney General]] [[Rod Rosenstein]]'s connection to Comey's firing, and her claim that "countless" FBI agents had lost faith in him. She repeatedly told the press that "countless members" of the FBI had contacted her to complain about Comey, but admitted to investigators that her claims were "a slip of the tongue" and "not founded on anything". When a redacted version of the special counsel's report was publicly revealed, Sanders defended herself, saying that her comments about the FBI agents were made in "the heat of the moment" and unscripted.<ref name=":4"/><ref>{{cite news |title=Sarah Sanders reiterates Comey claims despite admitting to lying |url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2019/apr/19/sarah-sanders-mueller-report-defends-lying-james-comey |access-date=April 21, 2019 |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=April 19, 2019}}</ref>

Sanders also lied about Trump's being in charge of a statement regarding the Trump Tower meeting. He worked on said statement with his advisor [[Hope Hicks]], and when the emails about that statement were made public, it was reported that he had helped with it himself.<ref name="VoxMueller">{{cite web |last1=Lind |first1=Dara |title=7 times the Mueller report caught Sean Spicer and Sarah Sanders lying to press |url=https://www.vox.com/2019/4/18/18485512/mueller-report-trump-lies-sarah-sanders |website=vox.com |publisher=[[Vox (website)|Vox]]|access-date=June 13, 2019 |date=June 13, 2019}}</ref><ref name=":3" /> According to the report, Sanders also made false statements about when Trump decided to fire Comey, as well as lying about Sessions's and Rosenstein's involvement in Comey's firing.<ref name="VoxMueller"/> ''The New York Times'' said that the revelation of false statements showed a "culture of dishonesty" at the [[White House]]. Of Sanders defending her comments on FBI agents, ''The New York Times'' wrote: "It has been a hallmark of the Trump White House never to admit a mistake, never to apologize and never to cede a point. This case was no different."<ref name=":4"/><ref name=":3"/>

Sanders's rhetoric about the report was found to be misleading or false. In March 2019, after Attorney General [[William Barr]] released a summary of Mueller's report on Russian interference in the 2016 election, Sanders falsely claimed that the investigation's findings were "a total and complete exoneration". The summary of the report stated it "does not exonerate him".<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://apnews.com/6d63e85b34dd44789ee27de4c3d61752|title=AP FACT CHECK: Mueller probe doesn't totally exonerate Trump|last=Yen|first=Hope|date=March 24, 2019|website=[[Associated Press|AP News]]|access-date=June 5, 2019}}</ref> Sanders repeated her false suggestion that the report exonerated Trump in May 2019, and falsely claimed that Mueller had "closed the case".<ref>{{Cite news |last=Yen|first=Hope|last2=Woodward|first2=Calvin|date=July 24, 2019|title=AP FACT CHECK: Trump falsely claims Mueller exonerated him|url=https://apnews.com/article/130932b573664ea5a4d186f752bb8d50|work=Associated Press}}</ref> The Associated Press noted, "Mueller did not fully exonerate Trump or declare that a possible case against Trump to be 'closed.' Mueller announced his work was finished specifically leaving it open for Congress to decide on possible charges of wrongdoing."<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://apnews.com/14e55be81c224c19ab57cdae81ec6826|title=AP FACT CHECK: Trump distorts findings of Russia probe|last1=Yen|first1=Hope|last2=Tucker|first2=Eric|date=June 1, 2019|website=[[Associated Press|AP News]]|access-date=June 5, 2019}}</ref>

== Career after the White House ==
[[file:Sarah Sanders (49290692076).jpg|thumb|right|Sanders giving a speech in 2019 after departing the Trump Administration]]
On August 22, 2019, [[Fox News]] announced that Sanders would become a contributor to the network, effective September 6.<ref name="FOXRelease">{{cite news |title=FOX NEWS SIGNS SARAH HUCKABEE SANDERS TO CONTRIBUTOR ROLE |url=http://press.foxnews.com/2019/08/fox-news-signs-sarah-huckabee-sanders-to-contributor-role/ |access-date=August 22, 2019 |work=[[Fox News]] |date=August 22, 2019}}</ref> During the December 2019 [[2020 Democratic Party presidential debates|Democratic presidential debate]], Sanders mocked [[Joe Biden]]'s stutter on Twitter. She apologized the next day.<ref>{{citation|website=[[Politico]]|title=Sarah Sanders apologizes after mocking Biden's stutter|date=December 20, 2019|first=Abbey|last=Marshall|url=https://www.politico.com/news/2019/12/20/sarah-sanders-joe-biden-stutter-088555}}</ref> Following the [[January 6 United States Capitol attack]], the editor of ''[[Forbes]]'' warned corporations against hiring Sanders and other Trump "propagandists", writing, "''Forbes'' will assume that everything your company or firm talks about is a lie."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lane |first1=Randall |author-link1=Randall Lane (journalist)|title=A Truth Reckoning: Why We're Holding Those Who Lied For Trump Accountable |url=https://www.forbes.com/sites/randalllane/2021/01/07/a-truth-reckoning-why-were-holding-those-who-lied-for-trump-accountable/ |access-date=January 10, 2021 |website=[[Forbes]]}}</ref>

=== 2022 Arkansas gubernatorial election ===
