On January 25, 2021, Sanders announced her candidacy for governor of Arkansas, an office her father, Mike Huckabee, held from 1996 to 2007.<ref>{{cite web |title=Sarah Sanders Running for Arkansas Governor |url=https://abc11.com/sarah-huckabee-sanders-governor-arkansas-donald-trump/9996735/ |website=[[Associated Press]] |access-date=January 25, 2021 |date=January 25, 2021 |archive-date=February 1, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210201194936/https://abc11.com/sarah-huckabee-sanders-governor-arkansas-donald-trump/9996735/ |url-status=dead }}</ref> A day later, Trump endorsed Sanders, calling her "a warrior".<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/meet-the-press/blog/meet-press-blog-latest-news-analysis-data-driving-political-discussion-n988541/ncrd1255678#liveBlogHeader|title= Trump rewards Sarah Huckabee Sanders with early endorsement for Arkansas gov|work=[[NBC News]]|last=Stahl|first=Chelsea|date=January 26, 2021}}</ref> On July 16, 2021, Sanders broke the Arkansas gubernatorial fundraising record by raising over $9 million.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://news.yahoo.com/sarah-huckabee-sanders-smashes-state-184110665.html|title=Sarah Huckabee Sanders Smashes State Gubernatorial Fundraising Record|website=[[Yahoo! News]]|date=July 16, 2021 |access-date=June 29, 2022}}</ref> Running during the COVID-19 pandemic, she pledged not to implement any mask mandates or vaccine mandates.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Castronuovo |first=Celine |date=July 23, 2021 |title=Huckabee Sanders vows not to impose mask, vaccine mandates if elected governor of Arkansas |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/campaign/564579-huckabee-sanders-vows-not-to-impose-mask-vaccine-mandates-if-elected/ |access-date=July 24, 2021 |website=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |language=en}}</ref> She also supported restrictions on abortion, saying that she would not support exceptions for rape and incest in anti-abortion legislation.<ref>{{Citation |title=Sarah Huckabee Sanders talks abortion laws & COVID-19 | date=September 7, 2021 |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lIsZK5PvcLg |language=en |access-date=September 4, 2022}}</ref>

In October 2021, the Arkansas Law Notes published an article that called into question whether Sanders was even eligible to run for governor.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://law.uark.edu/alr/lawnotes/articles/PDF-McLellandBaxter.pdf|title=An Open Governor's Seat, Open Constitutional Question, and the Need for an Answer|first1=Samuel Steele|last1=McLelland|first2=James R.|last2=Baxter|website=Law.mark.edu|access-date=June 29, 2022}}</ref> The article centers on whether Sanders meets the State Constitution's residency requirement of seven years. Sanders handily won the Republican primary and defeated Democratic nominee Chris Jones in the general election. On November 9, 2022, it was announced that Sanders had won the race and would become Arkansas's first female governor and the first woman to be governor of a state where her father was governor.<ref name="auto" /><ref name="auto1" />

== Governor of Arkansas (2023–present) ==
=== Tenure ===
Sanders was sworn in on January 10, 2023. On her first day in office, Sanders banned the term ''[[Latinx]]'' from being officially used in the state government.<ref>{{cite news |title=Arkansas Gov. Sarah Huckabee Sanders bans term 'Latinx' on her first day in office |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/news/latino/arkansas-gov-sarah-huckabee-sanders-bans-term-latinx-first-day-office-rcna65351 |access-date=January 12, 2023 |work=[[NBC News]] |language=en}}</ref>  The decision came after hearing feedback from Hispanic leaders in the state, state lawmakers and senior members of staff.<ref>{{Cite web |first1=Gustavo |last1=Valdes |first2=Nicole |last2=Chavez |date=January 12, 2023 |title=Arkansas Gov. Sarah Huckabee Sanders bans use of term 'Latinx' in state documents {{!}} CNN Politics |url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/01/12/politics/latinx-sarah-huckabee-sanders-order-arkansas-reaj/index.html |access-date=January 13, 2023 |website=[[CNN]]|language=en}}</ref> 

==== Abortion ====
In 2023, Sanders signed a bill to allow a privately funded anti-abortion "monument to the unborn" to be displayed on Capitol grounds. The monument will mark the number of abortions performed in Arkansas before the [[Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization|U.S. Supreme Court overturned ''Roe v. Wade'']].<ref>{{Cite web |date=March 20, 2023 |title=New law allows anti-abortion monument at Arkansas Capitol |url=https://apnews.com/article/huckabee-sanders-abortion-ban-arkansas-capitol-monument-9efa40bd10d3f3a1c6a9c70ed4986ae5 |access-date=March 20, 2023 |website=[[Associated Press|AP News]] |language=en}}</ref>

==== Education ====

===== LEARNS Act =====
On March 8, 2023, Sanders signed her signature education reform bill, the "LEARNS Act", into law. The law includes a number of provisions that would expand school choice, raise teacher pay, and ban teaching [[critical race theory]].

School choice expansion efforts included an Educational Freedom Account program that would expand access to school choice to all K-12 students after three years. The program provides up to 90% of the annual per-student public school funding rate for usage on education expenses, which includes private school tuition. It requires all participating schools to administer annual, state-approved testing, along with random annual audits of the participating schools' individual accounts. It includes provisions to remove limits on the number of charter schools, remove the 3% cap on school choice transfers unless a school district is under a desegregation order, and absorb the previously enacted Succeed Scholarship Program, which provided roughly $7,400 for disabled, foster, and military children.<ref name=":9">{{Cite web |last=Grajeda |first=Antoinette |date=March 9, 2023 |title=What is the Arkansas LEARNS Act? |url=https://arkansasadvocate.com/2023/03/08/how-will-the-learns-act-impact-arkansas-families/ |access-date=June 23, 2025 |website=Arkansas Advocate |language=en-US}}</ref>

The LEARNS Act also affects teacher pay and education policies. The state raised the starting teacher salary from $36,000 to $50,000 and required that teachers be paid at least $2,000 more for the 2023-24 school year, while eliminating the minimum salary increase schedule for teachers based on education level and experience. It created a fund to support minimum salary levels, but required school districts to use 80% of their funding allocated for salary increases before qualifying for state funding to support the increase; a provision was included allowing schools to apply for waivers of this policy if it would cause them to go into fiscal distress. LEARNS also repeals incentives for teacher recruitment in "high priority" districts and creates a Merit Teacher Incentive Fund that provides annual bonuses of up to $10,000 for eligible teachers based on merit performances.<ref name=":9" />

The LEARNS Act repealed the Teacher Fair Dismissal Act and the Public School Employee Fair Hearing Act, instead changing policy to specify that employees have a right to notice of a recommendation for termination from the public school district and a hearing before the school board. Superintendents may no longer hire employees based on seniority or tenure, and instead must consider only performance and effectiveness. Other stipulations include requiring superintendents to consult with teachers before hiring a principal and allowing school boards to employ one or more assistant superintendents.<ref name=":9" />

Provisions affecting student education and content include required literacy screenings for K-3 students and access to literacy coaches for K-3 students in D- and F-rated schools; students not meeting third-grade reading standards are to be held back from advancing to fourth grade the next year. Several prohibitions of content were implemented, including "indoctrination" or critical race theory, along with content that is sexually explicit or pertains to reproduction, gender identity, or sexual orientation before grade 5. Graduation criteria were changed, requiring students to have completed 75 hours of community service by the 2026-27 school year, allowing students to earn a diploma through a career-ready pathway rather than the traditional pathway, and allowing students in C- through F-rated schools to take courses not offered at their school elsewhere through the Course Choice Program.<ref name=":9" />

Other LEARNS Act provisions include a 50/50 cost-sharing policy between the state and school districts that allows teachers to take 12 weeks of maternity leave, along with changes to the student loan repayment process for teachers. It also requires eligible postsecondary institutions to create an Arkansas Teacher Academy to encourage people to enter the teaching profession. School Transformation Contracts were also created, allowing public school districts with D or F ratings to be exempt from a state takeover if they instead partner with an open enrollment charter school or other State Board of Education-approved entity. Early childhood education is also addressed, with requirements for the relocation and creation of Early Childhood Education offices and systems into the Arkansas Department of Education.<ref name=":9" />

===== Public response to the LEARNS Act =====
The passage of the LEARNS Act drew mixed reactions. State Senate Minority Leader [[Tippi McCullough]], a Democrat from Little Rock, expressed concern, saying, "LEARNS will dismantle and defund our public schools through a voucher system that has not worked anywhere ever. While some of the bill is admirable, its purported benefits will not reach our students in greatest need."<ref>{{Cite web |last=Grajeda |first=Antoinette |date=March 9, 2023 |title=Arkansas governor signs wide-ranging education bill into law |url=https://arkansasadvocate.com/2023/03/08/arkansas-governor-signs-wide-ranging-education-bill-into-law/ |access-date=June 23, 2025 |website=Arkansas Advocate |language=en-US}}</ref>

After the law went into effect, concerns rose about potential consolidation due to the bill. The [[Marvell–Elaine School District]] in rural [[Phillips County, Arkansas|Phillips County]] was scheduled to be consolidated under the Education Reorganization Act, which Governor Mike Huckabee signed into law in 2004, due to having fewer than 350 students and an F rating. State Senator [[John Payton (politician)|John Payton]], a Republican from [[Wilburn, Arkansas|Wilburn]] who voted for the LEARNS Act, proposed S.B. 292, which would eliminate the Education Reorganization Act's requirement that school districts whose two-year average daily attendance drops below 350 be consolidated. Sanders signed the bill into law after its passage on March 29, 2023. State Representative Mark McElroy, a Republican from [[Tillar, Arkansas|Tillar]] who also voted for LEARNS and whose district includes the Marvell-Elaine school district, proposed an additional bill that included exceptions for districts that are classified as in need of Level 5 – Intensive support and that have students who would have to ride a bus more than 40 miles to attend the new consolidated district.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Grajeda |first=Antoinette |date=April 6, 2023 |title=Bill to save Arkansas Delta school from consolidation awaits governor's signature |url=https://arkansasadvocate.com/2023/04/06/bill-to-save-arkansas-delta-school-from-consolidation-awaits-governors-signature/ |access-date=June 23, 2025 |website=Arkansas Advocate |language=en-US}}</ref> The Marvell-Elaine School District ultimately remained open under a $200,000 transformation contract authorized by LEARNS, with the Friendship Education Foundation Arkansas managing the district.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Grajeda |first=Antoinette |date=August 11, 2023 |title=Marvell-Elaine prepares to start the school year under Arkansas' first 'transformation contract' |url=https://arkansasadvocate.com/2023/08/11/marvell-elaine-prepares-to-start-the-school-year-under-arkansas-first-transformation-contract/ |access-date=June 23, 2025 |website=Arkansas Advocate |language=en-US}}</ref>

The Citizens for Arkansas Public Education and Students (CAPES) initiated an effort to repeal LEARNS. The Arkansas Constitution allows citizens to place a referendum of any act passed by the General Assembly on the ballot by petition. CAPES had roughly 55 days to gather signatures from 6% of the total votes cast for governor in the previous election along with 3% of voters in at least 50 counties each. CAPES fell about 500 signatures short of the 54,422 signatures needed by July 31, 2023, to place the referendum on the ballot.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Grajeda |first=Antoinette |date=August 1, 2023 |title=Arkansas LEARNS repeal group submits signatures but fears it fell short |url=https://arkansasadvocate.com/2023/07/31/arkansas-learns-repeal-group-submits-signatures-but-fears-it-fell-short/ |access-date=June 23, 2025 |website=Arkansas Advocate |language=en-US}}</ref>

==== Response to disasters and emergencies ====
On March 31, 2023, Sanders declared a state of emergency in Arkansas after a tornado outbreak hit the state. This activated the National Guard, which began assisting with the recovery effort. Sanders also announced that she had spoken to President Joe Biden, who promised Arkansas federal aid to assist with the recovery.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Moon |first=Melissa |date=March 31, 2023 |title=Four killed, dozens injured by tornado in Wynne, AR |url=https://wreg.com/news/wynne-high-school-damaged-by-tornado/ |access-date=June 28, 2023 |website=WREG.com |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=April 2, 2023 |last1=Davies |first1=Alys |last2=Mackintosh |first2=Thomas |title=US tornadoes: Death toll grows as extreme storms ravage several states |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-us-canada-65150138 |access-date=April 2, 2023 |website=[[BBC News]] |language=en-GB}}</ref> 

On April 5, 2024, Sanders declared a five-day<ref name=":8">{{Cite web |title=Sanders Releases Funds from the Response and Recovery Fund Ahead of the Eclipse - Arkansas Governor - Sarah Huckabee Sanders |url=https://governor.arkansas.gov/news_post/sanders-releases-funds-from-the-response-and-recovery-fund-ahead-of-the-eclipse/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240410052341/https://governor.arkansas.gov/news_post/sanders-releases-funds-from-the-response-and-recovery-fund-ahead-of-the-eclipse/ |archive-date=April 10, 2024 |access-date=April 16, 2024 |website=governor.arkansas.gov |language=en-US}}</ref> state of emergency in Arkansas ahead of the [[solar eclipse of April 8, 2024|solar eclipse of April 8]], releasing $100,000 from the state Response and Recovery Fund to assist with the state's response to the eclipse while waiving certain federal restrictions on commercial traffic for up to 14 days.<ref name=":8" /> Money from the disaster fund was to be used at the discretion of A.J. Gary, director of the Department of Public Safety's Division of Emergency Management, "to defray both program and administrative costs".<ref name=":8" /><ref>{{Cite web |date=April 5, 2024 |title=Sanders declares state of emergency in Arkansas ahead of eclipse {{!}} Arkansas Democrat Gazette |url=https://www.arkansasonline.com/news/2024/apr/05/sanders-declares-state-of-emergency-in-arkansas/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240410150029/https://www.arkansasonline.com/news/2024/apr/05/sanders-declares-state-of-emergency-in-arkansas/ |archive-date=April 10, 2024 |access-date=April 16, 2024 |website=www.arkansasonline.com |language=en}}</ref>

On March 14–15, 2025, severe storms impacted the state of Arkansas. Sanders declared a state of emergency and made a request for a major disaster declaration to the federal government, however, the request was initially denied by FEMA and President Trump. After an appeal from Sanders and Arkansas' Congressional delegation to reconsider, Trump approved the declaration on May 13, 2025.<ref>{{Cite web |date=May 13, 2025 |title=Gov. Sarah Sanders thanks Trump for Arkansas storm relief assistance |url=https://www.thv11.com/article/news/local/trump-issues-major-disaster-declaration-arkansas/91-fd6dfb8c-07d0-4393-ac7e-e3f132a9089a |access-date=June 23, 2025 |website=thv11.com |language=en-US}}</ref>

On April 5, 2025, a major disaster declaration request made by Sanders was approved by Trump and FEMA for severe storms and flooding that began on April 2, 2025.

==== Spending controversy and freedom of information ====
In June 2023, Sanders's office purchased a custom-made lectern for $19,029.25. The Arkansas Republican Party later reimbursed the governor's office for the lectern<ref name="podiumABC1">Cathey, Libby (October 25, 2023). [https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/sarah-huckabee-sanders-defends-podiumgate-after-unusual-19k/story?id=104263678 Sarah Huckabee Sanders defends herself from podium controversy after unusual $19K payment], ''[[ABC News (United States)|ABC News]]''</ref> several days after a [[Freedom of Information Act (United States)|Freedom of Information Act]] request for the purchasing documents was filed.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Betts |first=Anna |date=October 10, 2023 |title=In Arkansas, a $19,000 Lectern for the Governor Draws Scrutiny |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/10/10/us/sarah-huckabee-sanders-arkansas-lectern.html |access-date=January 19, 2024 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> An executive assistant was instructed to add the note "to be reimbursed" to the original invoice and leave the note undated.<ref>{{Cite web |date=October 10, 2023 |title=Scrutiny of Arkansas governor's $19,000 lectern deepens after new records are released |url=https://apnews.com/article/lectern-huckabee-sanders-arkansas-audit-invoice-dba58dd4f1c6cc8bd994052aa98798f5 |access-date=January 20, 2024 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref> After substantial media coverage of the purchase,<ref name="podiumAP1">DeMillo, Andrew (October 4, 2023). [https://apnews.com/article/huckabee-sanders-arkansas-lectern-audit-97d4173d485dd00291b1853ab339cf8a A $19,000 lectern for Arkansas Gov. Sarah Huckabee Sanders sparks call for legislative audit], ''[[Associated Press]]''</ref> the Arkansas State Legislature began an audit.<ref name="podiumABC1" /> Sanders denied any impropriety and said she welcomed the audit.<ref name="podiumAP1" />

The audit<ref>{{Cite web |title=Special Report Arkansas Legislative Audit Review of Selected Transactions and Procurements Arkansas Governor's Office For the Period May 1, 2023 through December 10, 2023 |url=https://arklegaudit.gov/downloadReport.php?id=SPSR00723 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240416005843/https://arklegaudit.gov/downloadReport.php?id=SPSR00723 |archive-date=April 16, 2024 |access-date=April 16, 2024}}</ref> showed that the lectern was purchased from Beckett Events LLC, a Virginia-based company run by political consultant and lobbyist Virginia Beckett, for $11,575. Beckett charged $2,500 for a "consulting fee" and $2,200 for the road case. The remainder of the cost represented shipping, delivery, and a credit-card processing fee.<ref name=":7">{{Cite news |last=Medina |first=Eduardo |date=April 15, 2024 |title=Audit Questions Purchase of $19,000 Lectern by Arkansas Governor's Office |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/04/15/us/arkansas-sanders-lectern-audit.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240416135806/https://www.nytimes.com/2024/04/15/us/arkansas-sanders-lectern-audit.html |archive-date=April 16, 2024 |access-date=April 16, 2024}}</ref> Similar lectern models are listed online for $7,500 or less, while the purchased lectern had no microphone or electronic components aside from a light. Sanders said it had additional features that contributed to its cost, including a custom height.<ref>{{Cite web |date=April 15, 2024 |title=Audit says Arkansas governor's office potentially violated laws with $19,000 lectern purchase |url=https://apnews.com/article/arkansas-huckabee-sanders-lectern-audit-19000-eb69927a5df693ac3e131b3c80f34b37 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240416031628/https://apnews.com/article/arkansas-huckabee-sanders-lectern-audit-19000-eb69927a5df693ac3e131b3c80f34b37 |archive-date=April 16, 2024 |access-date=April 16, 2024 |website=AP News |language=en}}</ref> The audit also found that a staff member had contacted an Arkansas-based equipment dealer and received a quote for portable podiums ranging in cost from $800 to $1,500.<ref name=":7" /> After Sanders proposed to deny public access to certain gubernatorial records, she faced public pushback. She later signed a law limiting public access to information about her security detail and her travels.<ref name="podiumAP1" />

==== Other issues ====
In October 2023, Sanders signed an executive order to eliminate the usage of "woke, anti-woman words" from official state documents.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Webb |first=Jack A. |date=October 19, 2023 |title=No 'womxn' allowed: Sanders signs order eliminating 'woke' words from Arkansas business |url=https://katv.com/news/local/national-culture-war-continues-in-arkansas-republican-governor-sarah-huckabee-sanders-signs-order-eliminating-woke-words-pregnant-people-person-mom-chestfeeding-breastfeeding-human-breast-milk-birthing-birth-laboring-menstruating-womxn-womyn-woman-women |access-date=October 23, 2023 |website=KATV |language=en}}</ref> Banned terms include "womxn", "birth-giver", and "chestfeeding". The order was criticized by the [[American Civil Liberties Union|ACLU]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=October 19, 2023 |title=Arkansas governor bans 'anti-women' words from use in state government |url=https://www.thv11.com/article/news/local/sarah-huckabee-sanders-woke-anti-women-words-ban/91-0d5971dc-3a75-42c3-abf3-fc0d90532126 |access-date=October 23, 2023 |website=thv11.com |language=en-US}}</ref> On March 18, 2025, Sanders signed the [[nitrogen hypoxia]] death penalty bill into law, making Arkansas the fifth state to allow execution by nitrogen hypoxia.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://apnews.com/article/executions-arkansas-nitrogen-sarah-huckabee-sanders-4af9ebd60f272f65a09e266444bb0831|title=Arkansas governor signs legislation allowing executions by nitrogen gas|date=March 18, 2025|work=Associated Press}}</ref>

In November 2023, Arkansas-based [[Cooks Venture]] shut down operations. It informed contract farmers that it lacked the funds to process or feed any chickens and would kill all ~1.3 million birds.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Mobley |first=Andrew |date=February 14, 2024 |title=Arkansas poultry company closure leaves farmers high and dry, 1.3 million birds euthanized |url=https://katv.com/news/local/arkansas-poultry-company-closure-leaves-farmers-high-and-dry-13-million-birds-euthanized-cooks-venture-senator-bryan-king-madison-county-carroll-dustin-maybee-chicken-department-of-agriculture-secretary-wes-ward-avian-influenza-bird-flu-patrick-fisk |access-date=July 26, 2025 |website=KATV |language=en}}</ref> On December 8, state senator [[Bryan King (politician)|Bryan King]] sent Sanders a letter asking her to declare a state of emergency over the [[animal welfare]], environmental, disease, and economic concerns of the killing of the birds.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Grajeda |first=Antoinette |date=December 9, 2023 |title=Arkansas state senator requests assistance for poultry farmers impacted by company closure |url=https://arkansasadvocate.com/2023/12/08/arkansas-state-senator-requests-assistance-for-poultry-farmers-impacted-by-company-closure/ |access-date=July 26, 2025 |website=Arkansas Advocate |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite letter|first=Bryan|last=King|subject=request for government debt assumption and disaster deceleration|recipient=Sarah Huckabee Sanders|url=https://arkansasadvocate.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/12/Senator-King-Cooks-Venture-Letter.jpeg|date=December 8, 2023}}</ref> Sanders denied the request, calling it contrary to the values of free and fair markets.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hoffman |first=Michael |date=December 14, 2023 |title=Company moves ahead with plans to euthanize hundreds of thousands of chickens across northern Arkansas |url=https://www.ky3.com/2023/12/14/company-moves-ahead-with-plans-euthanize-hundreds-thousands-chickens-across-northern-arkansas/ |access-date=July 26, 2025 |website=KY3 |language=en |quote=}}</ref>

==In popular culture==
At the 2018 [[White House Correspondents Dinner]], during a [[Roast (comedy)|comedy roast]] of the Trump administration, stand-up comedian [[Michelle Wolf]] delivered several pointed jokes about Sanders, including one that [[Michelle Wolf#2018 White House Correspondents' Dinner controversy|provoked heated controversy]]: 
<blockquote>I actually really like Sarah. I think she's very resourceful. She burns facts, and then she uses that ash to create a perfect [[Smokey Eyes|smoky eye]]. Like maybe she's born with it, maybe it's lies. It's probably lies.<ref>{{Cite news| url= https://www.vox.com/policy-and-politics/2018/4/30/17301436/michelle-wolf-speech-transcript-white-house-correspondents-dinner-sarah-huckabee-sanders| title= Wonder what Michelle Wolf said to make everyone so mad? Read it here.|work=[[Vox (website)|Vox]]|date=April 30, 2018|access-date=May 1, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news| url= https://www.foxnews.com/entertainment/michelle-wolf-doubles-down-on-sarah-sanders-insults-as-journalists-defend-press-secretary| title= Michelle Wolf doubles down on Sarah Sanders insults as journalists defend press secretary|last=Lam|first=Katherine|date=April 29, 2018|work=[[Fox News]]|access-date=May 3, 2018|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Citation|last=C-SPAN|title=Michelle Wolf – Complete Remarks at 2018 White House Correspondents' Dinner (C-SPAN)| date= April 28, 2018|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DDbx1uArVOM&t=14m03s|access-date=2018-05-06}}</ref></blockquote>

Some women in the press corps took offense at Wolf's joke, with [[Maggie Haberman]] of ''The New York Times'' tweeting, "That @PressSec sat and absorbed intense criticism of her physical appearance, her job performance, and so forth, instead of walking out, on national television, was impressive." MSNBC co-host [[Mika Brzezinski]] responded, "Watching a wife and mother be humiliated on national television for her looks is deplorable."<ref>{{cite web|url= https://x.com/morningmika/status/990585968825597954|title=Morning Mika Tweet|website=[[Twitter]]| date=April 29, 2018}}</ref>

Like several of her White House colleagues, Sanders was satirized on ''[[Saturday Night Live]]'',<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nbc.com/saturday-night-live/cast/aidy-bryant-14761/impersonation/sarah-huckabee-sanders-289435|title=Sarah Huckabee Sanders Sketches|website=[[NBC]]|access-date=July 21, 2017}}</ref> where she was portrayed by [[Aidy Bryant]]. In April 2023, the [[Anheuser-Busch]] brewing company hired [[TikTok]]er [[Dylan Mulvaney]], a [[Trans woman|transgender woman]] and activist, to promote [[Bud Light]]. Many conservative celebrities [[2023 Anheuser-Busch boycott|boycotted Bud Light]] as a result. Sanders supported the boycott by promoting "Real Women" beer can [[koozie]]s. On her [[Twitter]] account, she captioned a video, "Real women don't have to fake it." The koozies featured images of Sanders, [[Alabama]] governor [[Kay Ivey]], [[Iowa]] governor [[Kim Reynolds]], and [[South Dakota]] governor [[Kristi Noem]].<ref>{{cite web |last1=Sforza |first1=Lauren |title=Huckabee Sanders selling 'Real Women of Politics' koozies amid Bud Light backlash |url=https://thehill.com/homenews/3967209-huckabee-sanders-real-women-koozies/ |website=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] |date=April 24, 2023 |access-date=April 26, 2023}}</ref> Sanders's activities met with mixed media responses.<ref>{{cite magazine |last1=Otten |first1=Tori |title=Sarah Huckabee Sanders's Bud Light Ad Is So Bad It's Funny |url=https://newrepublic.com/post/172141/sarah-huckabee-sanderss-bud-light-ad-bad |magazine=[[The New Republic]] |date=November 2022 |access-date=April 26, 2023}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Spady |first1=Aubrie |title=Sarah Huckabee Sanders trolls Bud Light with koozie for 'real women' |url=https://www.foxnews.com/politics/sarah-huckabee-sanders-trolls-bud-light-koozie-real-women |website=[[Fox News]] |date=April 24, 2023 |access-date=April 26, 2023}}</ref>

==Personal life==
[[File:Mike, Janet, Sarah Huckabee Sanders, and Bryan Sanders.jpg|thumb|[[Mike Huckabee]], [[Janet Huckabee]], Sarah Huckabee Sanders, and Bryan Sanders in August 2025.]]
Huckabee met Bryan Sanders during her father's 2008 presidential campaign. She was the campaign's field director, and Sanders was hired as a media consultant. The couple married in 2010.<ref>{{cite news |last=Gibbs |first=Constance |date=May 10, 2017 |title=9 facts about deputy press secretary Sarah Huckabee Sanders |work=[[New York Daily News]] |location=[[New York City]] |url=http://www.nydailynews.com/news/politics/9-deputy-press-sec-sarah-huckabee-sanders-article-1.3153931 |access-date=July 22, 2017}}</ref><ref name="ADG">{{cite web |date=June 27, 2010 |title=Huckabee daughter weds in Virgin Islands ceremony |url=http://www.arkansasonline.com/news/2010/jun/27/huckabee-daughter-weds-virgin-islands-cer-20100627/ |url-access=subscription |access-date=March 5, 2017 |work=[[The Arkansas Democrat-Gazette]]}}</ref><ref name="JLemireAP07222017" /> They have three children.<ref name=Star/><ref>{{cite news |last=Klein |first=Betsy |date=July 27, 2017 |title=The spokeswomen: A low-key milestone as Sanders becomes White House press secretary |website=[[CNN]] |url=https://www.cnn.com/2017/07/27/politics/women-press-sarah-huckabee-sanders/index.html |access-date=March 7, 2018}}</ref><ref name=":6" /> 

On June 22, 2018, a co-owner of a 26-seat restaurant in [[Lexington, Virginia]], {{convert|200|mi}} from Washington, D.C., asked Sanders to leave the restaurant because she worked for the Trump administration, giving rise to the [[Red Hen restaurant controversy]].<ref name="WashPostSelk20180625">{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/local/wp/2018/06/23/why-a-small-town-restaurant-owner-asked-sarah-huckabee-sanders-to-leave-and-would-do-it-again/|title=The owner of the Red Hen explains why she asked Sarah Huckabee Sanders to leave|last1=Selk|first1=Avi|date=June 25, 2018|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180704024309/https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/local/wp/2018/06/23/why-a-small-town-restaurant-owner-asked-sarah-huckabee-sanders-to-leave-and-would-do-it-again/|archive-date=July 4, 2018|url-status=live|last2=Murray |first2=Sarah}}</ref> 

Sanders was diagnosed with [[papillary thyroid cancer]] in September 2022. She had surgery to remove her thyroid and the surrounding lymph nodes.<ref>{{cite news |last1=DeMillo |first1=Andrew |title=Sarah Sanders undergoes surgery for thyroid cancer |url=https://apnews.com/article/health-donald-trump-arkansas-cancer-mike-huckabee-195783f779f2eadec78abbb7cde9b64a |work=[[Associated Press|AP News]] |date=September 16, 2022 |language=en}}</ref> 

In April 2024, Sanders joined the [[Daughters of the American Revolution]].<ref>{{Cite tweet |number=1781421211920089414 |user=SarahHuckabee |title=Honored to be the first Arkansas Governor to be a member of the Daughters of the American Revolution and even more honored to be the first to address this event in 50 years. It takes constant work to protect our freedoms. Thankful we have the women of the DAR helping in the fight. |first=Sarah |last=Huckabee Sanders}}</ref>

